title: orbital_context
---
// The orbital station of the Science dimension (`specs/orbital_station/`):
// one node per resident. The errand of consignment 0447 (quest
// `orbital_consignment`) runs through Okafor at customs, Georg Canter in
// the Math town's Surveyor's Office (`math.yarn`), and back.
//
// Flags: `orbital_consignment_query` (Okafor asks for a signed count),
// `orbital_manifest_signed` (Canter signs it), `orbital_consignment_cleared`
// (Okafor clears the crate: the freight lane's gates open) and
// `orbital_spur_pass` (the hatch of the damaged spur opens).
//
// Never played. No placement names this node; it declares the zone.
===

title: station_okafor
---
<<if verdict("world.flag_set", "orbital_consignment_cleared") == "satisfied">>
    Officer Okafor: Lane's open. Crate 0447 went on the Remainder at shift change.
    -> Anything else held?
        Officer Okafor: Always. Not today's problem. Hamid wanted someone in the spur; your pass is on the hatch.
    -> Who sees your passenger lists?
        Officer Okafor: Customs. There was a request to sort them by home dimension and send them to another office. No signature on it. I sent it back.
    -> ‹step away›
<<elseif verdict("world.flag_set", "orbital_consignment_cleared") == "unanswerable">>
    <<stop>>
<<elseif verdict("world.flag_set", "orbital_manifest_signed") == "satisfied">>
    Officer Okafor: A signature from the Math town. "Twelve lenses. Finite." He underlined finite.
    -> Clear the crate.
        <<command "world.set_flag" "orbital_consignment_cleared" true>>
        <<command "world.set_flag" "orbital_spur_pass" true>>
        Officer Okafor: Stamped. The pen opens and the lane to the bay with it. One more thing: Hamid needs someone in the damaged spur who does not breathe. I put a pass on the hatch.
    -> Not yet.
<<elseif verdict("world.flag_set", "orbital_manifest_signed") == "unanswerable">>
    <<stop>>
<<elseif verdict("world.flag_set", "orbital_consignment_query") == "satisfied">>
    Officer Okafor: Still need a count I can stamp. Surveyor's Office, Math town. Ask for Canter.
    -> Where is the Math town?
        Officer Okafor: Through the ring and up the stairs from its gate hall. The office is on the second terrace.
    -> ‹step away›
<<elseif verdict("world.flag_set", "orbital_consignment_query") == "not_satisfied">>
    Officer Okafor: Customs. Crate 0447 in my pen is survey lenses from the Math town, for the Remainder. The manifest gives the count as "aleph-null". I cannot clear an infinite crate.
    -> I can ask the sender.
        <<command "world.set_flag" "orbital_consignment_query" true>>
        Officer Okafor: Surveyor's Office, Math town. Bring me a number I can stamp.
    -> Why so careful?
        Officer Okafor: Seed stock, spores, mail with ideas in it. The ring moves anything. Somebody has to look first.
    -> Who reads the arrival records?
        Officer Okafor: My shift and the checkpoint. Had a request from an office I'd never heard of. They can come down here and show me a badge.
    -> ‹step away›
<<else>>
    <<stop>>
<<endif>>
===

title: station_reyes
---
<<if verdict("world.flag_set", "orbital_consignment_cleared") == "satisfied">>
    Captain Reyes: 0447 is aboard. We burn for the survey moon at shift change. Thanks for fetching a number.
    -> Why does the moon need lenses?
        Captain Reyes: To map it. We need to find a place to land the next survey crew. If there is room for a permanent settlement, I'll probably be taking them out there for years.
    -> Have you flown near the gravity works?
        Captain Reyes: Once. Survey instruments gave me two horizons. I steered by the stars and stayed well clear.
    -> ‹step away›
<<elseif verdict("world.flag_set", "orbital_consignment_cleared") == "not_satisfied">>
    Captain Reyes: That pen on the gate deck holds my lenses. Without them the survey is blind.
    -> What do you haul?
        Captain Reyes: Whatever the ring brings that has to go where no ring is. Moons, mines, the slow places.
    -> Why not put a ring on the moon?
        Captain Reyes: It would need its own power plant and a crew to keep the gate running. We've only got a few people on that moon. I'm cheaper.
    -> What's the work out beyond the rings?
        Captain Reyes: Two weeks out, two back. We bring batteries, food, water. They send us rocks in labeled bags.
        Captain Reyes: It's a decent job, when customs lets us do it.
    -> ‹step away›
<<else>>
    <<stop>>
<<endif>>
===

title: station_lindqvist
---
Bo Lindqvist: Ring throughput is down a third since they doubled the passenger slots. Freight waits. I wait.
-> Why passengers first?
    Bo Lindqvist: The Authority added the evening passenger service. I've asked them to give me back the freight slot on Thursdays.
-> Need a hand?
    Bo Lindqvist: Not unless you can lift a container. ...You can? Huh. Union says no.
-> Can freight go another way?
    Bo Lindqvist: Send it by ship. I've priced it. Three weeks for a box that used to take a morning.
    Bo Lindqvist: Captain Reyes is already booked solid.
-> Who decides the schedule?
    Bo Lindqvist: The Authority's timetable office. I can send them a report on how long the freight waits. I can't make them read it.
-> ‹step away›
===

title: station_rahimi
---
<<if verdict("world.flag_set", "switch_orbital_spur_breaker_used") == "satisfied">>
    Hamid Rahimi: Power is back in the spur. The crawlers went quiet when the breaker closed. I owe you.
    -> What broke them?
        Hamid Rahimi: Their orders. "Repair anything damaged." The storm damaged everything. They kept cutting.
    -> Is the rest of the station all right?
        Hamid Rahimi: Better than yesterday. The gravity-works people want their instruments back now. They can collect them themselves.
    -> ‹step away›
<<elseif verdict("world.flag_set", "switch_orbital_spur_breaker_used") == "unanswerable">>
    <<stop>>
<<elseif verdict("world.flag_set", "orbital_spur_pass") == "satisfied">>
    Hamid Rahimi: Okafor logged your pass. The spur took a storm last cycle. Breaker's tripped at the far end.
    -> What is in there?
        Hamid Rahimi: No air, live cable under the deck, and maintenance crawlers that do not stop. Reset the breaker if you can.
    -> ‹step away›
<<elseif verdict("world.flag_set", "orbital_spur_pass") == "not_satisfied">>
    Hamid Rahimi: Service deck. Don't touch anything that hums.
    -> What is behind the hatch?
        Hamid Rahimi: The old spur. Damaged. Shut until customs signs off on someone with a pass and no lungs.
    -> How much power does the ring take?
        Hamid Rahimi: Opening it, all of it. Holding it, half. That is why we schedule.
    -> Have you worked at Gnu-ton's place?
        Hamid Rahimi: Inspected a power feed once. Spent an hour holding onto my tools. They've got floors facing every direction.
        Hamid Rahimi: If you go out there, take a good grip on whatever you bring.
    -> ‹step away›
<<else>>
    <<stop>>
<<endif>>
===

title: station_pell
---
Gus Pell: First time through the ring? People climb that ladder green. Crackers by the galley.
-> I don't get sick.
    Gus Pell: A robot. Lucky. Some folks lose a day to it. Some just lose breakfast.
-> What's cooking?
    Gus Pell: Greens from the racks, and whatever came through the gate this morning. Today, beans.
-> Who lives here?
    Gus Pell: Mechanics, pilots, customs, a botanist who talks to the lettuce. Most stay for a few months.
    Gus Pell: Some go home every night now. I still set their place at breakfast by accident.
-> ‹step away›
===

title: station_wen
---
Wen Liang: I tuned this station's first stable ring. The first link held three seconds. Now people commute.
-> What changed?
    Wen Liang: We can hold the link for a lot less power than the old design took. The hard part now is finding a crew to look after it.
-> Do you miss the work?
    Wen Liang: Sometimes. Hamid lets me watch when they do the calibration. I can usually tell if the gate is out of tune by ear.
-> Ever worked with Gnu-ton?
    Wen Liang: He sent me a plan for a gravity test. I asked where the shutoff was. He sent me a longer plan.
    Wen Liang: I've still got it on my desk.
-> How many dimensions are there?
    Wen Liang: The registries list seventeen known dimensions. Math Town sent over a question about two of the entries.
    Wen Liang: I sent measurements back. Now they've asked for more.
-> ‹step away›
===

title: station_neil
---
Neil onGras Turfson: Well, actually, that blue line on the edge is all the air they have. Thinner than paint on a ball.
-> It looks fragile.
    Neil onGras Turfson: It is. Look at it long enough and you will want to go down and touch some grass.
-> Do you ever go down there?
    Neil onGras Turfson: Every leave. I take my shoes off on the first bit of grass I see. Last time it rained on me and I stayed outside till they called.
-> Do you know Will Force?
    Neil onGras Turfson: The horse on QED-TV? I call in from this window when he gets the sky wrong. Well, actually, he rarely gets it wrong. I call in anyway. The audience likes it.
    Neil onGras Turfson: Last month he said the station "floats". It falls. It falls all the time, and it moves sideways fast enough that it keeps missing the ground. He thanked me on air and then asked if I had a comb.
-> What's beyond this orbit?
    Neil onGras Turfson: Remainder goes out surveying. I work the observation window. You should ask the captain where she's been.
-> ‹step away›
===

title: station_dobbs
---
Dobbs: Welcome to orbit! First time up the ribbon? You look like a first time. Shirt? Globe? Air?
-> What is in the bottles?
    Dobbs: Genuine orbit air. Bottled at thirty-five thousand, seven hundred and eighty-six kilometres. Nothing but the finest vacuum.
    Dobbs: Well. Not vacuum. The vacuum kept escaping. It's station air. But it was bottled up here. That's the genuine part.
-> What is in the snow globes?
    Dobbs: The planet. Shake it and there's weather. It's educational.
-> Do people really buy this?
    Dobbs: Mostly visitors. Last week one of the pilots bought a globe for her daughter. She wanted me to mark her orbit on it.
-> Just looking.
    Dobbs: Go ahead. The big window is past the kiosk.
===

title: works_pippin
---
Hortensia Pippin: Mind the apples. They fall up here. I've been picking this orchard thirty years and I still look the wrong way first.
-> How do you pick an apple that falls up?
    Hortensia Pippin: Long pole and a lot of patience. I put a net over the tree and loosen the fruit with the pole. If you move the net, I'll spend all afternoon on the ceiling.
-> Why is that tree upside down?
    Hortensia Pippin: That one grew toward the light from the planet. The rest grew toward the lamps. I keep asking the engineers to leave the lamps in one place.
-> Do you know GNU-ton?
    Hortensia Pippin: He planted the first tree. Sat under it, waiting for an apple to fall on his head. Took him a week to think of looking up. Now he won't stop lecturing about it.
-> I'll let you work.
    Hortensia Pippin: Take one from the crate. Hold it tight. If you let go, it's the ceiling's.
===
